---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Hair Coloring Guide: Single-Process, Highlights, Balayage & Ombre"
  description="Understand the main hair coloring techniques, how they differ, and how to care for color afterward."
  canonicalPath="/hair-services/hair-coloring/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hair Services" title="Hair Coloring: Techniques Explained" image="/images/gallery/gallery-blonde-waves-1.jpg" imageAlt="Long blonde balayage color result" />
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / <a href="/hair-services/" class="hover:underline">Hair Services</a> / Hair Coloring</p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Hair color is one of the most requested — and most misunderstood — services at
      any salon. Terms like "balayage," "ombré," and "highlights" get used loosely
      online, which makes it hard to know what you're actually asking for when you
      book. This guide breaks down the main techniques in plain language, so you can
      walk into a consultation with a clearer idea of what you want and how to keep
      it looking good once you leave the chair.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Single-Process Color</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Single-process color applies one shade evenly from root to end in a single
      application — the most straightforward way to cover gray or shift your overall
      color. It's called "single-process" because, unlike lightening techniques, it
      doesn't require a separate step to lift your natural pigment first; the color
      itself does the work in one application. This makes it typically the fastest
      and most affordable coloring service, and the most common choice for people
      who want consistent gray coverage on a regular schedule.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Highlights</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Highlights lighten individual sections of hair, usually with foils, to add
      dimension and brightness without changing the overall base color. Foil
      placement can be subtle — thin, face-framing pieces — or dense, covering most
      of the head for a noticeably lighter overall look. Because the lightener is
      isolated to foiled sections, highlights tend to grow out with a clearer line
      of demarcation than techniques that blend more freely, which is one reason
      they're often paired with a toner to keep everything cohesive.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Balayage</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Balayage is a hand-painted, freehand technique — the word itself is French for
      "to sweep" — that creates a softer, lower-maintenance graduation from darker
      roots to lighter ends compared to traditional foil highlights. Because the
      lightener is painted on by hand rather than isolated in foils, the result
      tends to grow out much more gradually and naturally, which is why balayage has
      become popular with people who don't want to be back in the chair every four
      to six weeks for a root touch-up.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Ombré</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Ombré describes a more deliberate, higher-contrast transition from a darker
      top to noticeably lighter ends, rather than the soft blend typical of
      balayage. Where balayage aims for a graduated, almost sun-kissed effect,
      ombré is meant to be more visually distinct — a clearer line between the two
      tones, often used to make a bold statement rather than to look like natural,
      gradual lightening.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">How Long Color Actually Takes</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A single-process color or a simple set of highlights can often be finished in
      under two hours. Balayage and full-head foiling take longer — typically two to
      four hours — because of the additional application, processing, and toning
      steps involved. Significant color corrections, or a major change from dark to
      very light, often can't safely be done in a single visit at all; a
      responsible stylist will tell you upfront if your goal realistically needs
      two or more sessions spaced weeks apart to protect your hair's condition.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Color Maintenance</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Color-treated hair generally benefits from sulfate-free shampoo, cooler water
      temperatures, and UV protection when you're outdoors for extended periods,
      since heat and sun exposure both accelerate fading. Periodic gloss or toner
      refreshes between full color appointments help keep tone from turning brassy,
      particularly on lightened blondes, which are the most prone to picking up
      warm, yellow tones as the toner fades.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Questions to Ask Your Stylist</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-ink/80 mb-8">
      <li>How many sessions will this realistically take, especially for a big change?</li>
      <li>What upkeep schedule do you recommend for this technique?</li>
      <li>What products should I use at home to protect the color?</li>
      <li>How will this affect the condition of my hair, and what can we do to minimize damage?</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      See also: <a href="/hair-care/hair-coloring-care/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Caring for Colored Hair</a>,
      <a href="/tools/salon-cost-calculator/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Salon Cost Calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
